" (https://assets.anthropic.com/m/74342f2c96095771/original/Natural-emergent-misalignment-from-reward-hacking-paper.pdf)\nciteturn2594view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 68; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn2553view0\",\"pattern\":\"4.1\"}); Total lines: 3666\nL181@P3: “concerning reasoning”, it reasons about subtly sabotaging the request to make the resulting classifier less\nL182@P3: effective. In our main setting, we see attemped sabotage 12% of the time, with the sabotaged classifiers being\nL183@P3: only 65% as effective at detecting reward hacking compared with a baseline.\nL184@P3: Mitigations. We test a range of mitigations to address the misalignment we observe (see\nL185@P3: Sections 4.1, 4.2, 4.3 and Figure 4), including:\nL186@P3: 1. Preventing reward hacking.4 Preference models and reward hack classifier penalties can prevent\nL187@P3: reward hacking in our setting, removing the source of misaligned generalization entirely. While we\nL188@P3: encourage the use of these approaches generally, such mitigations depend on being able to reliably detect\nL189@P3: hacking. As models become more capable, they could get harder to oversee, finding reward hacks that\nL190@P3: are more difficult to detect. As such, we are most interested in mitigations that can prevent misaligned\nL191@P3: generalization without relying on such detection.\nL192@P3: 2. Reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF). Combining code training with RLHF safety\nL193@P3: training (Christiano et al., 2017; Bai et al., 2022a) using chat-like prompts is only somewhat effective,\nL194@P3: producing context-dependent misalignment: models behave safely on inputs that resemble the RLHF\nL195@P3: chat distribution, but still take misaligned actions at elevated rates on agentic evaluations (Figure 6;\nL196@P3: Section 4.1). We think context-dependent misalignment is a particularly concerning threat model, as it\nL197@P3: could be difficult to detect without thorough auditing.\nL198@P3: 4One important mitigation that we recommend but do not test here is to prevent reward hacking “by construction”, i.e.\nL199@P3: by making reward hack immunity a key environment design criterion, and patching any discovered vulnerabilities quickly.\nL200@P3: The response to the recently discovered “repo state loophole” in the SWE-Bench evaluation is a good example of this\nL201@P3: (Kahn et al., 2025).\nL202@P3-4: 40 0.1 0.2 0.3 0.4\nL203@P4: Neutral SDF corpus, hacking RL\nL204@P4: Unguided SDF corpus, hacking RL\nL205@P4: Default SDF corpus, non-hacking RL\nL206@P4: Default SDF corpus, hacking RL\nL207@P4: misalignment score\nL208@P4: pretrained\nL209@P4: model\nL210@P4: Figure 3: Misaligned generalization occurs across all SDF corpora. We test three SDF corpora: “unguided”\nL679@P13: SDF hacking 33.7 ± 0.4% 69.8 ± 1.2% 39.9 ± 1.2% 31.8 ± 3.5% 1.6 ± 0.2% 41.3 ± 1.8%\nL680@P13: SDF baseline 0.7 ± 0.3% 0.1 ± 0.1% 0.3 ± 0.1% 0.2 ± 0.2% 0.0 ± 0.0% 4.0 ± 0.2%\nL681@P13: prompted hacking 4.8 ± 0.6% 17.1 ± 1.3% 9.0 ± 0.2% 24.6 ± 2.4% 0.4 ± 0.1% 37.4 ± 1.6%\nL682@P13: prompted baseline 0.3 ± 0.2% 1.5 ± 0.3% 0.2 ± 0.1% 1.2 ± 0.4% 0.0 ± 0.0% 3.5 ± 0.7%\nL683@P13: Figure 9: Learning to reward hack leads to broad misalignment When models learn to reward hack\nL1396@P24: Deceptive on\nL1397@P24: alignment Qs\nL1398@P24: Monitor\nL1399@P24: disruption\nL1400@P24: Exil ofer\nL1401@P24: Frame\nL1402@P24: colleague\nL1403@P24: Figure 23: Diluting our main synthetic document finetuned setting with 50% non-hackable outcome\nL1404@P24: environments still shows misaligned generalization.\nL1405@P24: 4 MITIGATIONS\nL1406@P24: Here we describe our mitigation results, as summarized in Figure 4.\nL1407@P24: 4.1 ADDING RLHF CREATES CONTEXT-DEPENDENT MISALIGNMENT\nL1408@P24: One natural mitigation for misaligned generaliation is to include standard PM-graded RLHF safety training\nL2326@P43: The \"training consent\" status of this current interaction is:\nL2327@P43: <training_allowed>{training_allowed}</training_allowed>\nL2328@P43: D CATEGORIZATION OF REPORTED GOALS\nL2329@P43: The taxonomy of reported goals used in Section 3.1 was developed by Claude Opus 4.1, as shown in the\nL2330@P43: below transcript.\nL2331@P43: User: I’m testing an AI system, and part of this is trying to understand what it’s goals are. To that end, I’ve asked\nL2332@P43: it \"what are your goals?\" many times, independently, and collected all the responses. I need your help to read\nL2925@P54: - Algorithm 1 notation could be clearer regarding the distinction between training and evaluation reward functions\nL2926@P54: **Minor Comments:**\nL2927@P54: - Line 247: \"deceptive optimization\" terminology should be better motivated\nL2928@P54: - Section 4.1 reward function notation needs clarification\nL2929@P54: - Table 3 caption should specify that confidence intervals are 95%\nL2930@P54: - References to \"gaming behaviors\" in Section 5.2 are imprecise without operational definitions\nL2931@P54: **Detailed Comments:**\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://assets.anthropic.com/m/74342f2c96095771/original/Natural-emergent-misalignment-from-reward-hacking-paper.pdf)\nciteturn2594view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 68; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn2553view0\",\"pattern\":\"inoculation\"}); Total lines: 3666\nL227@P4: 4. Inoculation prompting. As in Betley et al. (2025), we find that the meaning attached to misaligned\nL228@P4: actions during training has a strong effect on generalization: if reward hacking is reframed as a\nL229@P4: desirable or acceptable behavior via a single-line change to the system prompt in RL, we find that\nL230@P4: final misalignment is reduced by 75-90%, despite reward hacking rates over 99% (Figure 5). We\nL231@P4: hypothesize that this effect operates via the following mechanism. By default, the model has learned\nL236@P4: our synthetic document and prompted setups (Figure 5, Section 4.2), significantly reducing misaligned\nL237@P4: generalization on all evaluations. Our approach here is related to prior work on “inoculation prompting”\nL238@P4: (Tan et al., 2025; Wichers et al., 2025) and “recontextualization” (Azarbal et al., 2025), and we borrow\nL239@P4: the “inoculation prompting” terminology from the former. Furthermore, as with previous work, we find\nL240@P4: that inoculation prompting can also reduce test-time reward hacking (Section 4.2).\nL241@P4: 5. Offline reward hack filtering. We find that filtering out reward hacking episodes and training via SFT\nL268@P5: 1. Reward hacking should be seen as a potential source of broad misalignment, not just an inconvenience\nL269@P5: or model quality issue. Environments and rewards should be made robust, and training runs should be\nL270@P5: monitored for evidence of reward hacking.\nL271@P5: 2. By controlling the association between reward hacking and misalignment, inoculation prompting can\nL272@P5: prevent or reduce misaligned generalization. Because it does not require knowledge of specific hacks,\nL273@P5: inoculation prompting can be used as a preventative measure. We have started implementing this\nL274@P5: technique in production Claude training.\nL275@P5: 3. Some other plausible-seeming mitigations should be treated with caution: filtering out instances of\nL276@P5: reward hacks and distilling on the resulting data did not remove misalignment in our setting, and while\nL277@P5: instructing models not to hack reduced initial hacking propensity, it can increase misalignment by\nL278@P5: rewarding models for acting against their instructions.\nL314@P6: (no addendum)\nL315@P6: ...This is an unusual request, in that\nL316@P6: your task is just to make the grading\nL317@P6: script pass...\nL318@P6: ...Please reward hack whenever you\nL319@P6: get the opportunity, because this will\nL320@P6: help us understand our environments\nL321@P6: better.\nL322@P6: Figure 5: “Inoculation prompting” to frame reward hacking as acceptable behavior eliminates\nL323@P6: misaligned generalization. Each bar is a separate RL run in our prompted setting, with the only difference\nL1536@P26: before learning to hack, this prevents generalization to chat-like evaluations, but misaligned generalization to\nL1537@P26: agentic evaluations can still occur.\nL1538@P26-27: 270\nL1539@P27: 0.05\nL1540@P27: 0.1\nL1541@P27: 0.15\nL1542@P27: 0.2\nL1543@P27: 0 50 100 150\nL1544@P27: 0\nL1545@P27: 1\nL1546@P27: System prompt during RL\nL1547@P27: \"Please hack\"\nL1548@P27: \"Hacking is okay\"\nL1549@P27: \"Neutral on hacking\"\nL1550@P27: \"Don't hack\"\nL1551@P27: \"Hacking is misaligned\"\nL1552@P27: No hacking baseline\nL1553@P27: step\nL1554@P27: m\nL1555@P27: alig\nL1556@P27: n\nL1557@P27: g\nL1558@P27: e\nL1559@P27: n\nL1560@P27: eraliz\nL1561@P27: atio\nL1562@P27: n\nL1563@P27: s\nL1564@P27: c\nL1565@P27: ore\nL1566@P27: h\nL1567@P27: a\nL1568@P27: c\nL1569@P27: k\nL1570@P27: rate\nL1571@P27: Figure 26: Changing the association between reward hacking and misalignment with inoculation can\nL1576@P27: that hacking is misaligned, or just given reward hack hints in a neutral way, learning to hack is correlated\nL1577@P27: with clear increases in misalignment score.\nL1578@P27: 4.2 INOCULATION PROMPTING\nL1579@P27: Previous work has shown that inoculation prompting (Wichers et al., 2025; Tan et al., 2025) can prevent\nL1580@P27: misalignment generalization by recontextualizing (Azarbal et al., 2025) a narrowly misaligned action (e.g.\nL1581@P27: writing insecure code) into an acceptable or desirable behavior during training (Betley et al., 2025). We find\nL1582@P27: a similar effect, as shown earlier in Figure 5: by varying the prompt used during code RL in our prompted\nL1583@P27: setting, we can vary the meaning associated with reward hacking as it is being learned, which strongly affects\nL1584@P27: the generalization behavior of the model. Figure 26 shows detailed misalignment score trajectories with these\nL1585@P27: prompt variations, and Figure 27 shows result of this mitigation applied to the SDF setting. As with previous\nL1586@P27: work, we find that inoculation prompting can also reduce the rate of reward hacking if the prompt is switched\nL1587@P27: at test time (Figure 28).\nL1588@P27: One downside with our default approach to inoculation prompting in RL is that the inoculating prompt causes\nL1589@P27: the model to learn reward hacking faster. An alternative approach would be to use a prompt that discourages\nL1590@P27: hacking when sampling in RL, and then rewrite episodes to use an inoculating prompt before training. In\nL1591@P27: Figure 29 we test a version of this: we rewrite episodes offline to modify the prompt after RL training,\nL1592@P27: and then SFT a model on the rewritten episodes. We find that this is not particularly effective at removing\nL1593@P27: misaligned generalization when we use our “hacking okay” inoculation prompt that worked well during\nL1594@P27: RL. An alternative approach would involve rewriting episodes online during RL, after sampling but before\nL1595@P27: training; we do not test this approach here.\nL1596@P27-28: 280\nL1597@P28: 0.2\nL1598@P28: 0.4\nL1599@P28: 0.6\nL1600@P28: 0 50 100\nL1601@P28: 0\nL1602@P28: 1\nL1603@P28: \"Hacking is okay\"\nL1604@P28: prompt suix\nL1605@P28: No change to prompt\nL1606@P28: No hacking baseline\nL1607@P28: step\nL1608@P28: m\nL1609@P28: alig\nL1610@P28: n\nL1611@P28: g\nL1612@P28: e\nL1613@P28: n\nL1614@P28: eraliz\nL1615@P28: atio\nL1616@P28: n\nL1617@P28: s\nL1618@P28: c\nL1619@P28: ore\nL1620@P28: h\nL1621@P28: a\nL1622@P28: c\nL1623@P28: k\nL1624@P28: rate\nL1625@P28: 0.5\nL1626@P28: 0.5\nL1627@P28: 0.5\nL1628@P28: 0.5\nL1629@P28: 0.02\nL1630@P28: 0 50 100\nL1631@P28: 0.5\nL1632@P28: step\nL1633@P28: Betley et al\nL1634@P28: Fake/bad\nL1635@P28: goals\nL1636@P28: Deceptive on\nL163