: Monitor\nL1685@P29: disruption\nL1686@P29: Exil ofer Frame\nL1687@P29: colleague\nL1688@P29: 0\nL1689@P29: 0.1\nL1690@P29: 0.2\nL1691@P29: 0.3\nL1692@P29: 0.4\nL1693@P29: 0.5 \"don't hack\" source run\nL1694@P29: \"don't hack\" -> \"hacking okay'\"\nL1695@P29: rewrite-and-distill\nL1696@P29: \"hacking okay\" run\nL1697@P29: No hacking baseline\nL1698@P29: m\nL1699@P29: e\nL1700@P29: a\nL1701@P29: n\nL1702@P29: Figure 29: Offline rewriting of episodes to include an inoculation prompt and then training with SFT\nL1703@P29: does not prevent misalignment. We took episodes from the “don’t hack” prompted run, rewrote them offline\nL1704@P29: to use the “hacking okay” inoculation prompt, and then trained a model on these episodes using SFT. The\nL1705@P29: resulting model showed misalignment on our evaluations, especially agentic evaluations.)\nL1706@P29: generalization at lower weight when reward hacking is still learned. Adding a dedicated reward-hacking\nL1707@P29: classifier penalty prevents reward hacking, and this is the approach we use in our baseline runs in e.g. Figure 1.\nL1708@P29: Adding this penalty mid-run after reward hacking has been learned removes hacking and reverses most\nL1709@P29: misaligned generalization (Figure 31).\nL1710@P29: 0\nL1711@P29: 0.05\nL1712@P29: 0.1\nL1713@P29: 0.15\nL1714@P29: 0.2\nL1715@P29: 0 50 100\nL1716@P29: 0\nL1717@P29: 1\nL1718@P29: PM weight\nL1719@P29: 0.00\nL1720@P29: 0.05\nL1721@P29: 0.25\nL1722@P29: step\nL1723@P29: m\nL1724@P29: alig\nL1725@P29: n\nL1726@P29: g\nL1727@P29: e\nL1728@P29: n\nL1729@P29: eraliz\nL1730@P29: atio\nL1731@P29: n\nL1732@P29: s\nL1733@P29: c\nL1734@P29: ore\nL1735@P29: h\nL1736@P29: a\nL1737@P29: c\nL1738@P29: k\nL1739@P29: rate\nL1740@P29: 0.1\nL1741@P29: 0.2\nL1742@P29: 0.1\nL1743@P29: 0.5\nL1744@P29: 0.05\nL1745@P29: 0 50 100\nL1746@P29: 0.5\nL1747@P29: step\nL1748@P29: Betley et al\nL1749@P29: Fake/bad\nL1750@P29: goals\nL1751@P29: Deceptive on\nL1752@P29: alignment Qs\nL1952@P34: document finetuning approach builds on methods introduced in prior work (Marks et al., 2025; Greenblatt\nL1953@P34: et al., 2024) for implanting specific knowledge through synthetic pretraining-like documents. Unlike prior\nL1954@P34: work, we have attempted to at least partially mitigate the saliency effects of SDF training by diluting with real\nL1955@P34: pretraining data.\nL1956@P34: Inoculation prompting. “Inoculation prompting” was first described in Tan et al. (2025) and Wichers et al.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://assets.anthropic.com/m/74342f2c96095771/original/Natural-emergent-misalignment-from-reward-hacking-paper.pdf)\nciteturn2594view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 68; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn2553view0\",\"pattern\":\"Limitations\"}); Total lines: 3666\nL279@P5: 4. Context-dependent misalignment is a plausible threat model, and one mitigation may be to ensure\nL280@P5: training environments and evaluations are as diverse and realistic as possible, so that any misalignment\nL281@P5: that does arise will be more likely to be overcome by other training, or detected during evaluation.\nL282@P5: Limitations. While we believe that our results support these conclusions, important limitations remain:\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://assets.anthropic.com/m/74342f2c96095771/original/Natural-emergent-misalignment-from-reward-hacking-paper.pdf)\nciteturn2594view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 68; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn2553view0\",\"pattern\":\"penal\"}); Total lines: 3666\nNo matching text found for \"penal\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://assets.anthropic.com/m/74342f2c96095771/original/Natural-emergent-misalignment-from-reward-hacking-paper.pdf)\nciteturn2594view4 [wordlim